What I'm trying to say is that, if it was exposed in flake, we can do something like:

```
outputs = args: {
  packages.x86_64-linux.my-custom-override = inputs.nixpkgs.nixos.release.imagePath.overrideAttrs (...);
}
```

whereas currently there's no way to access it in nix expressions, as release.nix is not exposed in any way, not even through [default.nix in nixpkgs/nixos](https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/tree/master/nixos). I'm not sure how would you be editing it via `-E` either, since its not in default.nix, you can't import it as `let release = import <nixpkgs/nixos/release> in release.{path}`...

And I don't think flakes are by far any less flexible to do the same thing, rather the enforcement makes them more standardized to work with consistent api.
